WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.640
France in flames.
 
We have all seen these images.&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.960
For several days at the end of June and the&nbsp;
beginning of July, the French nation once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.700
experienced a situation of complete chaos.
 
The footage of protests, looting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.960
fire and riots that broke out in France's&nbsp;
major cities were seen around the world.
 &nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.760
Of course, these images are no longer something&nbsp;
extraordinary in this country. Events and images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.020
more or less similar, are repeated every&nbsp;
so often and with increasing intensity.
 &nbsp;

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.740
It doesn't matter if we are talking about&nbsp;
racial or religious vindication and protests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.100
the final of the soccer "Champions&nbsp;
League", the yellow vests or pension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.780
reforms. Violence is becoming a distinctive&nbsp;
sign of any social movement in this country.
 &nbsp;

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:47.700
The latest wave of riots triggered by the fatal&nbsp;
shooting of 17-year-old Nahel Merzouk by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.680
policeman resulted in more than 5,000 vehicles&nbsp;
burned, more than 1,000 properties damaged or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.620
looted, 269 police stations attacked and more&nbsp;
than 4,000 people arrested. It was madness.
 &nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.940
Even so, let's not fool ourselves, because&nbsp;
these protests will not go down in history.
 &nbsp;

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.100
In today's France, the latest wave of violence&nbsp;
will be just that, the last wave before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.240
next one comes in the not too distant future.
 
It is something that is bound to happen... And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.360
it will probably happen sooner than we can&nbsp;
imagine. Because VisualPolitik community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.380
France is starting to be broken, the&nbsp;
country is in serious danger of implosion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.020
and every now and then the flames rise up.
("It is as if France is a pressure cooker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.660
[each crisis] reveals tensions, a conflict in&nbsp;
society... Our country is constantly invoking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.860
republican values, but it seems that&nbsp;
whole segments of the population do not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:43.200
feel that this matters to them" – Bruno Cautrès,&nbsp;
political researcher in declarations to Politico)
 &nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.140
The situation has reached such a&nbsp;
point that, during the protests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.060
French President Emmanuel Macron went so&nbsp;
far as to advocate ideas such as these:&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.840
(Macron suggests blocking social&nbsp;
media during riots – Le Monde)
 &nbsp;

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.400
But what exactly is going on in France?&nbsp;
Why does society seem completely broken?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.320
What is behind these huge waves of violence?&nbsp;
What consequences are they likely to have?
 &nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.820
VisualPolitik viewers, in this video we are going&nbsp;
to tell you which are the three main factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.260
in all this chaos that every now and then is&nbsp;
unleashed in France. The three major ingredients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.820
that will allow us to better understand exactly&nbsp;
what is happening in France and why Paris and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.660
other major French cities are becoming the&nbsp;
Gotham Cities of Europe. But in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:28.260
obviously there’s no Batman in sight... Although,&nbsp;
as we will see, some characters pretend to be.
 &nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:36.060
Let's get on with it.
(THE BANLIEUES: THE FUSE)
 &nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.300
In order to really understand this&nbsp;
story we have to start by going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.260
back to the middle of the 20th century.
 
As in many other large cities in Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:51.900
and America, Paris and other large French cities&nbsp;
were expanding at full speed. Industrialization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.800
and the exodus from the countryside to the&nbsp;
city were multiplying urban populations.
 &nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.160
In the case of France, this&nbsp;
phenomenon was also accelerated by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:06.600
decolonization processes of its former empire.
 
In response to the overcrowding of cities and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.940
appearance of shantytowns and substandard housing,&nbsp;
the suburbs of cities began to grow in the 1960s:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.520
the famous "banlieues"
They did so in Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.240
Lyon, Marseilles and other large cities.
 
They were developed under the direction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.780
the French State itself, which began to&nbsp;
promote thousands and thousands, tens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.860
thousands of social housing units in these new&nbsp;
neighborhoods where, of course, schools, medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.720
centers and public transportation lines were&nbsp;
also opened. On paper the idea was fantastic.
 &nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.660
With these new neighborhoods and the thousands&nbsp;
of public housing units built by the government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.480
the poorest French could not&nbsp;
only have access to a house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.000
but also to a much more affluent lifestyle.
 &nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.240
However, problems soon arose. You&nbsp;
know that there is a massive gap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.560
between theory and practice and that good&nbsp;
intentions do not guarantee good results.
 &nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.940
Well, initially, most of the housing was&nbsp;
destined for the French who had emigrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.540
from the countryside to the city, but as soon&nbsp;
as they could, they moved to neighborhoods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.260
considered better. So what happened to&nbsp;
those vacated State housing spaces? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.100
they were acquired by or passed into&nbsp;
the hands of the immigrant population.
 &nbsp;

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:12.060
At the same time, the deindustrialization&nbsp;
that Paris has experienced over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.020
four decades has closed the doors to&nbsp;
good jobs and good salaries for many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.820
of the residents of these same neighborhoods.
 
And so, in this way, these areas ended up becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:24.480
something like a synonym of poverty,&nbsp;
immigration and all kinds of problems:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.680
school dropouts, discrimination, unemployment,&nbsp;
poorly maintained buildings, delinquency...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.060
Let's just say that little by little these&nbsp;
suburbs started to become real ghettos.
 &nbsp;

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.840
This was a particularly intense phenomenon for&nbsp;
many groups of immigrants from the Caribbean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:45.000
Senegal, Ivory Coast, Morocco and especially&nbsp;
Algeria, who have ended up living in separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.320
neighborhoods, where the cultural pressure of&nbsp;
their countries of origin is maintained and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.700
where levels of integration with French society&nbsp;
are relatively low. In recent years, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.360
many people from countries such as Pakistan&nbsp;
have been arriving. And, as you can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.100
the combination of such a rigid culture with&nbsp;
a ghetto environment is a ticking time bomb.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.400
Now, don't think that this has&nbsp;
taken anyone by surprise. In 1977,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.580
the French government was already&nbsp;
concerned that ghettos were forming.
 &nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.800
What's more, in the last 20 years alone, the&nbsp;
French State has spent a whopping 60 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.820
euros on a colossal campaign to renovate&nbsp;
housing blocks, improve infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.360
and upgrade all kinds of facilities in&nbsp;
these neighborhoods, particularly in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.620
poorest ones where more than five million&nbsp;
people live, half of them from Algeria.
 &nbsp;

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.820
In fact, during the latest protests, I am sure you&nbsp;
have all seen how libraries, schools and health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.540
centers were burned. And logically, if they&nbsp;
have been burned, it is because they exist.
 &nbsp;

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.460
(Health is free, school is free, and&nbsp;
sometimes we have the feeling that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.480
is never enough." – Emmanuel Macron)
 
On top of that, for years there have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.200
also been lots of organizations paid by the&nbsp;
government to try to make things better.
 &nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.320
Yet, despite all the efforts, the&nbsp;
results have been rather poor.
 &nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.140
The banlieues are widely known for&nbsp;
their high rates of unemployment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.800
school dropouts, drug addiction, poverty&nbsp;
and delinquency. It is a real disaster.
 &nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.680
Because many of the people who live&nbsp;
in these places, especially children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.060
do not have an easy time escaping from them.
 
And the fact is that changing material things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.120
is relatively simple. All you have to&nbsp;
do is pull out your checkbook. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.740
the same is not true of culture or people.
 
Do you want an example? Well, think of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.520
schools. Schools in these neighborhoods have&nbsp;
very high failure rates and their academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.600
level tends to be much lower. This means that&nbsp;
many students end up falling by the wayside.
 &nbsp;

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:32.520
Well, the fact is that this public housing policy&nbsp;
that ended up promoting real ghettos is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.540
key factors for explaining why France has&nbsp;
so many problems integrating immigrants.
 &nbsp;

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.920
Basically, immigrants arrive in France, they live&nbsp;
in France, their children are born in France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.600
but they never really become French.&nbsp;
Neither them nor their children. At least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.340
not completely. Here the data are compelling.
 
For example, it is estimated that about 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:54.420
of the children in these ghettos are poor.&nbsp;
Unemployment is almost double the national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:58.800
average and, of course, family incomes are&nbsp;
much lower. To give you an idea, one out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.460
five foreigners living in France thinks they are&nbsp;
discriminated against because of their origin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:06.600
their race or their religion. This is one of&nbsp;
the highest rates in the developed world.
 &nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.740
It turns out that France is one of the countries&nbsp;
that has the most problems integrating immigrants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.760
And these neighborhoods are largely to blame.
 
You don't believe me? Well...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:20.520
Take a look at this graph.
("There is still a feeling in these neighborhoods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.960
and probably a reality, that you don't have&nbsp;
as many opportunities as in other places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.340
when you see your parents and older siblings&nbsp;
struggling to find work. [...] We are paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.720
for the concentration of poverty, the lack of&nbsp;
diversity in the schools and the refusal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:39.000
build social housing outside these neighborhoods"&nbsp;
– Olivier Klein, Minister of Housing and Urban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.640
Affairs and former mayor of Clichy-sous-Bois, a&nbsp;
municipality in the eastern suburbs of Paris)
 &nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.020
Logically, all these factors generate&nbsp;
frustration. And, make no mistake, frustration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:57.000
is the best possible fuel for violence.
 
To make matters worse, two new problems have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.960
arisen in these neighborhoods in recent years:
 
Firstly, drug trafficking.
 &nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.560
(Cocaine supply at an all-time&nbsp;
high in France – LeMonde)
 &nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.640
Drug consumption in France has grown a&nbsp;
lot in recent years. It is estimated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.440
cocaine alone has more than 600,000 regular&nbsp;
users in France. This has obviously led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.640
the emergence of many underworld groups that&nbsp;
operate precisely from these neighborhoods.
 &nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.840
At the same time, there is the problem of&nbsp;
religious fundamentalism. It is precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.740
in these types of suburbs that most of the&nbsp;
Muslim population of France lives. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.160
think about it, the lack of opportunities, the&nbsp;
feeling of not belonging and the frustration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.180
have formed the perfect breeding ground&nbsp;
when it comes to recruiting fanatics.
 &nbsp;

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:37.080
For example, I don't know if you remember, but&nbsp;
before the pandemic, jihadist attacks in France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:41.700
were the order of the day. Well, most of them&nbsp;
were perpetrated by people of Algerian origin who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.200
lived, guess where? Exactly, in the banlieues.
 
This has logically contributed to an enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.460
spiral of distrust, violence and suspicion that&nbsp;
has only exacerbated the problem. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:54.120
people of foreign origin living in these&nbsp;
neighborhoods are 20 times more likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:58.680
to be stopped by police. Take a moment&nbsp;
to process that: 20 times more likely.
 &nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.920
Keep in mind that it’s a police force that&nbsp;
is not always particularly friendly. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.160
brings us directly to the second&nbsp;
major factor in this whole story.
 &nbsp;

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:11.700
Listen up.
(THE POLICE: THE ACCELERANT?)
 &nbsp;

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:19.740
The police force is by far one of the most&nbsp;
powerful institutions in France. Among other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:23.520
reasons because it is strongly unionized.
 
The police have a lot of political influence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:26.880
And in recent years the rise of jihadist&nbsp;
fundamentalism, the increasingly common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.940
protests against the government of the&nbsp;
day and the increase in drug trafficking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.200
has given them even more presence in society.
 
To give you an idea, for every year since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.280
beginning of the century, France has practically&nbsp;
passed a new security law that strengthens the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:41.880
police. For example, they can use intermediate&nbsp;
weapons in their patrols in the suburbs, something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.420
that is extremely rare in the rest of Europe.&nbsp;
Another example? They have been given the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:50.100
to stop and identify absolutely anyone they&nbsp;
want to without any reason whatsoever.
 &nbsp;

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.020
And their powers even include that, since 2017,&nbsp;
traffic police have been allowed to use lethal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:59.700
force against any driver fleeing a checkpoint.
 
In fact, although it may surprise you, Nahel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:04.860
Merzouk's case was not unprecedented. On 7 June,&nbsp;
just three weeks before Nahel's death, the Paris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:09.420
police killed a young woman, 21 years old, who&nbsp;
had allegedly refused to stop at a checkpoint.
 &nbsp;

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:14.400
On 14 June, two weeks before Nahel's death,&nbsp;
police also killed a 19-year-old delivery worker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:18.840
a Guinean boy, at another checkpoint.
 
In total, in just the 18 months prior to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:25.260
the death that triggered the protests, the French&nbsp;
police had killed 17 people at traffic controls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:26.920
It was madness.
 
And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.280
that's not all. In general, we are&nbsp;
talking about a police force that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:36.780
accumulates a lot of complaints for excesses&nbsp;
of all kinds and that, as you can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:41.760
is very unpopular in the suburbs.
 
And vice versa. According to polls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.800
police officers disproportionately&nbsp;
identify with parties like Marine Le Pen's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.580
which are calling for a much firmer hand.&nbsp;
In fact, after the latest riots broke out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:55.380
the two big police unions employed a tremendously&nbsp;
inflammatory tone. They went so far as to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:00.900
that they were at war against hordes of savages&nbsp;
and that they had to put an end to the rats.
 &nbsp;

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.540
Well, what can I say? You can&nbsp;
have whatever opinion you like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:09.480
but I don't know, statements like that don't&nbsp;
exactly seem likely to inspire calm, do they?

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:14.940
The French police are no joke.
 
In fact, keep in mind what Macron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:19.560
himself was saying in 2017:
("I want to change the culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:25.020
management style and recruitment of the&nbsp;
police force" – Emmanuel Macron in 2017)
 &nbsp;

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:29.040
Nevertheless, not long after came the yellow&nbsp;
vests, the pension reform protests and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:32.640
on... And naturally, Macron wanted nothing&nbsp;
to do with fighting with the police... And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.280
especially not with his unions.&nbsp;
It was the last thing he needed!
 &nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.280
Of course, the fact that the police are&nbsp;
incapable of controlling the suburbs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:42.300
that the population of these neighborhoods has so&nbsp;
little confidence in them only adds fuel to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:46.440
problem of the ghettos that we have already seen.
 
But hold on just a moment, because in this entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:51.420
story not everyone loses out. The third major&nbsp;
factor in this whole mess... It is nothing short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:56.460
of the great beneficiary of this whole story.
 
Any ideas? Well... Stay tuned.&nbsp;

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.540
(CLAMOR FOR AN IRON FIST: THE CONSEQUENCE)
 &nbsp;

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:11.220
The latest wave of violence has resulted&nbsp;
in one clear winner: Marine Le Pen.
 &nbsp;

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:15.900
The French are starting to be fed up and Le&nbsp;
Pen has capitalized on the moment like no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:20.160
else. Le Pen and her party took advantage of the&nbsp;
riots to attack the Macron government, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.720
to present a package of heavy-handed measures&nbsp;
intended to please the majority of voters."
 &nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.680
For example, the National Rally, formerly&nbsp;
the National Front, Le Pen's party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:33.240
wants young people from 16 years old to be able&nbsp;
to be tried in criminal proceedings as adults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:37.800
it wants those convicted of crimes to lose the&nbsp;
right to live in public housing or to receive any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:41.880
kind of payment or consideration from the State.&nbsp;
It also wants many more people to be imprisoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:45.420
and to reduce social payments to people living&nbsp;
in these very suburbs we are talking about.
 &nbsp;

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:49.620
And do you know what? All this is starting&nbsp;
to sound good to the French... Remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.000
that’s not us saying it. On 30 June a&nbsp;
poll was conducted which showed that Le&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.720
Pen's response has been the best rated&nbsp;
of all political leaders in France.
 &nbsp;

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:02.220
("The scum we see looting stores,&nbsp;
torching libraries and city halls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:07.020
all while laughing, are not doing this for&nbsp;
Nahel. It is an excuse to attack the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.480
and everything that represents the republic."&nbsp;
– Edwige Diaz, deputy of the National Rally)
 &nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.620
Despite everything, support for the police&nbsp;
has remained quite high among the French.
 &nbsp;

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.700
According to a poll published&nbsp;
by the newspaper Le Figaro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:28.620
57% sided with the police, while 69%&nbsp;
supported declaring a state of emergency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:33.600
which was, by the way, another of Le Pen's&nbsp;
demands. It’s been quite a political rally.
 &nbsp;

